body:
We're at 298 lessons, with 29 published. Four more chemistry lessons were published through the new publish loop.

- **The 20 QA failures were false.** The QA run went against an old build from before the 10 newest math and 10 newest economics lessons were added, so those pages returned 404. That happened because my rebuild had been killed along with my cleanup command.
- **What I did about it:**
  - deleted those 20 records without committing them;
  - rebuilt with all 298 lessons and started a fresh server;
  - pointed the economics reviewer at the fresh server;
  - started QA on the 30 lessons that still have no record (those 20, plus the 10 newest computer science lessons).
- **Checklist change:** before any QA or walkthrough run, the checklist now has a step to confirm the build contains every lesson. The incident is logged in the launch review.

Still running: builders for geography, probability, AI and chemistry, the physics and probability revisers, and reviewers for life sciences and economics.

Fact-check pass 7 is done and committed. It covered 45 lessons (geography, probability, economics and the priority items), verified 191 claims and disputed none. Open claims dropped from 606 to 415. Two small text fixes are on the board: change "about 3.5 °C" to "about 3.4 °C", and an area source line.

I've started two fact-checkers on the remaining 415 open claims, split by domain so they can't touch each other's files:

- one on AI, computer science and math;
- one on chemistry, life sciences, physics, probability and economics.

Also running:
- Leonie's role evaluation;
- the reviewer on the Sonnet-built lessons;
- the two queue builders that take us past 500.

We're at 496 lessons.

You are Tess, the Life Hacks category manager. Repo: /home/user/system-3-company (work in place, do NOT commit, do NOT touch company/ops/board.json). Two checks came back on your 5 briefs in company/domains/briefs/lifehacks.md:
- the safety review: company/reviews/<slug>/2026-10-13-safety.md for each slug (wobbly-table-quarter-turn passed with changes);
- the source check: company/reviews/lifehacks-briefs/2026-10-13-source-check.md.
Apply every required change to the briefs, and to the illustration requests in company/design/illustration-requests/. In particular:
- remove the cup from the wobbly-table hero request;
- present 35° only as the theorem's limit, adding "four feet down is not level", and add the stage line "Clear hot drinks before you turn it";
- correct the Martin reference and narrow its claim to square tables;
- state the Raymer & Smith setup;
- for the knot paper, use always-fails vs about half, not "twice as long";
- drop Cepeda 2006 from spaced-beats-cram claim 2, and give the reveal line "retrieving it again is what makes it stick" a planned claim and source;
- give textbook editions and sections.
Remove the "proposed; brief safety review pending" comments where the safety review passed. Leave wobbly-table's line in company/ops/brief-skips.json: the safety reviewer removes it after re-checking. Append a short "Changes applied" note to each safety record. Run `npm run check`. Report in under 120 words.

You are Tomoko, the design engineer of System 3 Company. The founder clarified the HQ design tool: "for concepts I was meaning UI concepts, not exact solutions; these look to belong to the category management". The tool you built at /hq/design is organised by curriculum concept, so it belongs to the category managers. The designers need a tool organised by UI concept: lesson formats, interaction patterns and visual styles.

Read: CLAUDE.md, company/roles/design-engineer.md, company/design/formats.md, company/design/system.md, company/design/illustration.md, src/kit/controls.tsx, src/kit/Lesson.tsx (read only; constitutional), and your existing code: src/lib/hq-design.ts, src/app/hq/design/*, src/app/hq/HQ.tsx and the HQ nav.

1. **Move the current tool to the category managers.** Move it to /hq/curriculum: tab "Curriculum", subtitle "Category management: concepts, lessons, verdicts". Rename the files and routes to match (/api/hq/curriculum/concept/[key]). /api/hq/sheet stays shared. Keep its deep links working.
2. **Build the new Design inspector at /hq/design**, organised by UI concept. Data lives in company/design/concepts/ (one `<id>.md` per UI concept, with frontmatter: id, name, kind = format | interaction | visual, status = proposed | approved | kit-supported | retired, owner, kit = component or "by hand"). Seed it from formats.md (the 8 formats) and system.md, plus interaction patterns you find in real lessons. Examples: slider-drives-live-chart, step/run-button simulation, drag-to-place, choice-before-reveal, toggle-before-after, scrubber timeline, two-panel shared control, tally counters, map projection switch. Add visual concepts from system.md: chart style, colour roles, typography scale, motion, illustration hero. The body of each file covers: when to use, when not to, anatomy, rules, open questions. Mark seeded entries "seeded by design-engineer; design lead to confirm".
3. For each UI concept, the inspector shows:
   - the description;
   - a **specimen**: a small live React rendering of the pattern with dummy data, built with existing kit controls where possible, or else an SVG wireframe;
   - the lessons that use it, detected from Demo.tsx (kit imports and JSX components used, e.g. Slider, Toggle, RunButton, WorldMap, two-panel layouts by heuristic). Show counts and a grid of their contact sheets (artifacts/walkthroughs/<slug>/sheet.png via /api/hq/sheet), so designers see how one pattern looks across many lessons;
   - review findings that touch the pattern (layout, told-not-shown, contrast/tap-size from the accessibility records in company/reviews/*/2026-10-13-accessibility.md if present), linked;
   - its variants side by side: compare 2–3 lessons, or 2 concepts, e.g. "storyboard vs multi-card".
   Filters: kind, status, kit support, has findings. Also a "proposals" view: concepts with status proposed, and a button-free note on how to propose one (add a file and a mockup in company/design/specs/).
4. Keep the HQ look in light and dark, make it work on a phone, gate it with hqEnabled(), and validate slugs as before. No new npm dependencies.
Run `npx tsc --noEmit`, `NEXT_DIST_DIR=.next-uic npx next build` (restore tsconfig.json afterwards with `git checkout tsconfig.json`) and `node tools/company.mjs check`. Take a few screenshots with Playwright (Chromium at /opt/pw-browsers/chromium) into /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/3e472d64-8589-5167-8584-c47a01ab4321/scratchpad/uic/ (NOT into the repo). Use tools/serve.sh to start and stop servers; never use pkill. Commit in your worktree branch, don't push, and don't touch company/ops. Report in under 250 words: routes, the number of seeded concepts by kind, how lesson usage is detected and how accurate it looks, and what the design lead must confirm.

You are the interaction designer of System 3 Company. This is the first time a designer is in the lesson process (operating loop step 3). Repo: /home/user/system-3-company (work in place, do NOT commit, do NOT touch company/ops/board.json, and do NOT write anything under company/design/concepts/: the design engineer is creating that folder now).

Read: CLAUDE.md, company/roles/interaction-designer.md, company/ops/operating-loop.md (step 3), company/design/formats.md, company/design/system.md, company/product/demo-standard.md, src/kit/controls.tsx, and the reference lessons that formats.md names for each format. Look at their walkthrough sheets at artifacts/walkthroughs/<slug>/sheet.png where they exist, to see what works on the stage.

Write a spec at company/design/specs/<slug>.md for each of these briefs, in build order:
- Life Hacks (company/domains/briefs/lifehacks.md): towel-spread-dries-first, spaced-beats-cram, square-bow-stays-tied, long-cord-knots-itself.
- Economics: mild-preferences-sharp-divides (company/domains/briefs/economics.markets.md). This uses the new agent-based simulation format, so design it carefully: it is the first one.
A spec is what the builder follows. It covers:
- the format, and why it fits;
- the stage layout at 390×844, top to bottom;
- every control: its kind, label, range or default, and order;
- what the stage shows at predict, during explore (each step or key state), and at the reveal;
- the milestones;
- what makes the payoff *shown, not told* (the most common review finding: 104 of 289 learner reviews say "told, not shown");
- the colour roles (use the design tokens and do not rely on colour alone);
- tap targets ≥ 44 px;
- the QA contract note: all controls must exist at first render;
- where an illustration slot goes, for Life Hacks (the Illustration component in src/kit).
Include a rough SVG wireframe for each spec at company/design/specs/<slug>.svg (one frame per phase is fine). Keep each spec to about one page. Run `npm run check`. Report in under 200 words: the 5 specs, the decisions you made that a builder might not expect, and anything the design lead must approve.

All three requests are handled. The book work is queued to start once the current review waves finish.

**1. Networks, Crowds, and Markets (Easley & Kleinberg, 2010).** It's on the board (W-137) for the principal PM for verticals, as the first Vertical Review:
- **Portfolio map:** map the book's 24 chapters to concepts and assign them across categories:
  - graphs and strong/weak ties to CS and Math;
  - games, auctions, matching and bargaining to Economics;
  - information cascades and power laws to Probability;
  - small worlds and epidemics to Life and Probability;
  - voting to Economics.
- **Deduplicate:** check the map against lessons we already have. Several overlap, e.g. Schelling (chapter 4), herd immunity and the small-world topics.
- **Briefs:** category managers write briefs that cite the chapter. Builders build after the design specs.
- We teach from the book's ideas and never copy its text or figures. The book is registered in `content/works.json` with all 24 chapters.

**2. Metadata on every lesson, so lessons can connect to the curriculum.** Lessons already had concept, misconception, objective, what the learner knows and doesn't, and a recall question. The new standard is in `company/product/lesson-metadata.md`:
- A **curriculum block**:
  - the primary concept;
  - other concepts it teaches, in any domain;
  - prerequisites, level and sub-area;
  - **references to books and courses**, down to chapter and section;
  - tags.
- **format** and **interactions**, which link to the UI concepts in the Design inspector.
- **`tools/meta.mjs`:**
  - `backfill` filled the curriculum block for all **509 lessons** from the curriculum files, never overwriting anything a person set.
  - `lint` lists gaps. The only field still missing everywhere is format, which the design engineer will fill from the inspector's detection (on the board).
  - `coverage --work=easley-kleinberg-2010` shows chapters × lessons, so we can track how much of the book we've covered.
- Category managers' briefs and builders' instructions now require these fields for every new lesson.

**3. Engineering floor.** HQ now has **Floor 5 · Engineering**:
- the **App team** room: Noor (platform: app shell, feed, kit) and Amara (backend);
- **Data & privacy**: Elif;
- the **Engineering review room**, for Kenji's reviews;
- **Release & deploy**.

Floor 1's old Engineering and Backend rooms are now a staircase to floor 5. Reporting lines are unchanged, so no consultation was needed. The move is announced on the HQ timeline so your directs see it.

**Still waiting on you:** the 15 lessons that passed QA but are held. Either **(a)** tell me to lift those holds, or **(b)** let the release manager edit the holds file himself. Either way, published goes from 125 to 140. Learner review batches 6–8, the knot library, the algorithm lessons and the phone layout fix are still running.

You are a demo builder in System 3 Company, wave C: revising lessons from learner reviews so they can launch. Read CLAUDE.md, company/roles/demo-builder.md, company/product/demo-standard.md and company/product/lesson-metadata.md. Work in your worktree and commit there (don't push). Never use pkill: use tools/serve.sh.

Your lessons are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/3e472d64-8589-5167-8584-c47a01ab4321/scratchpad/wc-builder-1.txt (domain/slug). The first 7 are FATAL; do them first. For each lesson:
1. Read the latest learner review in company/reviews/<slug>/ (*learner-adult-beginner*.md), plus any fact-check or safety record. Fix what it says:
   - leaks: options or captions that give the answer away, or float artefacts like 2.9999999999999996;
   - stage and reveal mismatches: the reveal must quote what the learner actually did or saw, never values from a fixed path they didn't visit;
   - told-not-shown payoffs: make the stage show the payoff;
   - undefined terms;
   - edge cases: zero, empty or reset states must never produce NaN or Infinity text.
2. Phone reveal clipping (W-135) is fixed in the kit, so don't fix clipping lesson by lesson unless the review says the lesson caps its own height.
3. Bump the version in meta.json. Every claim you add or edit goes to status "unverified", with text matching the stage exactly. Keep `curriculum` and add `format` if missing.
4. All controls must exist at first render. Run lesson QA (`QA_ONLY=<slugs> npm run qa` after `npm run build`; the feed-chips test failing under QA_ONLY is a known harness issue, W-136) and `npm run check`. Look at the walkthrough sheet for each lesson you changed (`npm run walkthrough -- <slug>`).
5. Append a short "## Revision v<N>" note to company/reviews/<slug>/build.md, or create it: what you changed, per finding.
Aim for all 15; stop cleanly if you run low. Report in under 200 words: the commit hash, the slugs done, and any finding you disagreed with and why.

